AI Mode conversation: create requirement.txtYou said: create requirements.txtcreate requirements.txtTo create a requirements.txt file for your Python project, you can use a few different methods depending on whether you are using a virtual environment or want to scan your code automatically. [1] (https://stackoverflow.com/questions/31684375/automatically-create-file-requirements-txt), [2] (https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python/how-to-create-requirements-txt-file-in-python/)Method 1: Using pip freeze (Recommended for Virtual Environments)If you are developing your project inside an isolated virtual environment (venv or conda), you can snapshot all currently installed packages directly to a text file. [1] (https://stackoverflow.com/questions/50777849/from-conda-create-requirements-txt-for-pip3), [2] (https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python/how-to-create-requirements-txt-file-in-python/)Open your terminal in your project's root folder.Run the following command:bashpip freeze > requirements.txt
Use code with caution. [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Tzda0Q2zfPA&t=22), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GK0usm20xes), [3] (https://stackoverflow.com/questions/31684375/automatically-create-file-requirements-txt)Method 2: Using pipreqs (Best for Global Environments)If you did not use a virtual environment, pip freeze will dump every single Python package installed on your computer. To avoid this, use pipreqs to scan your code and only list packages you actually import. [1] (https://towardsdatascience.com/python-pipreqs-how-to-create-requirements-txt-file-like-a-sane-person-c82da5d5cd13/), [2] (https://github.com/bndr/pipreqs), [3] (https://stackoverflow.com/questions/31684375/automatically-create-file-requirements-txt)Install the tool:bashpip install pipreqs
Use code with caution.Generate the file by pointing it to your current project folder:bashpipreqs .
Use code with caution.(Note: If the file already exists, use pipreqs . --force to overwrite it). [1] (https://towardsdatascience.com/python-pipreqs-how-to-create-requirements-txt-file-like-a-sane-person-c82da5d5cd13/)Method 3: Manual CreationIf your project only has a few dependencies, you can create a file named requirements.txt manually in your root directory and type out your packages. You can format them with or without strict versions: [1] (https://docs.posit.co/connect-cloud/how-to/python/dependencies.html), [2] (https://www.jetbrains.com/help/pycharm/managing-dependencies.html), [3] (https://docs.dominodatalab.com/en/cloud/user_guide/9c4f82/use-requirements-txt-python-only/)textnumpy==1.26.4
pandas>=2.0.0
